1 Introduction 
 
The HUD in the on-board AR-HUD system [1] uses the optical system to project the original image observed by the driver onto the front windshield of the car. This process undergoes complicated optical conversion and the non-standard face shape of the front windshield of the vehicle. The displayed image will have spatial position changes and severe distortion, which makes the cast virtual image form completely different from the original image. Therefore, it is necessary to perform calibration and pre- distortion processing on the projected image so that the driver can see that the virtual image is the same as the original image. At present, the existing technology selects the best observation position as a fixed observation point in the eye-box observable by the human eye, thereby performing virtual image imaging and distortion correction, but this method is less flexible. Because the driver’s head position and the dynamic changes of the human eye will cause the observer’s eye position space coordinates to be constantly changing, if the algorithm only considers the best typical position, when the human eye is up, down, left, right, front and back, it will greatly affect the imaging effect of the AR-HUD virtual image. The virtual image distortion correction method based on deep learning can achieve good imaging results in both calibrated and non-calibrated eye positions through learning, and its portability is strong and does not rely on proprietary tools. Once a model with ideal accuracy is obtained through iterative training, the model can be used to quickly obtain the correct AR- HUD imaging. 
